[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-29637"] :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add or subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-29664/selection/c3e74ce6fba7b7bd77c7afbf20a82e8f73f6cac3363858b8840c65664e418e03" :answer/id #uuid "67c8944a-bc97-5cd9-8b49-1007571d465e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\,561" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29664/selection/5172a7fa160e878951e4187c255e106b57da0d14098cfe25d30cce3dbd3535c3" :answer/id #uuid "7a5de78d-8204-59b0-97b6-53f125f809c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\,731" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29664/selection/48ed8822e91c61d2180f4e9f6c1b03af7fc6ed780f3ab54702820deafad2d24e" :answer/id #uuid "268946e7-5527-5a92-a28e-53fdc72568b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\,741" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29664/selection/93ba270014b2121e0d07324bf08630808d6ed9bfb1e523439708be4e1492051c" :answer/id #uuid "7738e148-e6ef-532b-a769-a0c953fd2cdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\,631" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29664/selection/af3979f69e3c75cfe7f3145a4d599cb170a6cd2ea2b65bc8aeb427117c37252c" :answer/id #uuid "0c9e7c40-836a-5dad-81aa-97c4e3cb76f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\,851" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29664/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2de1b9ec-2ce3-5f43-aef0-d2d472c22c7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-29664/selection/48ed8822e91c61d2180f4e9f6c1b03af7fc6ed780f3ab54702820deafad2d24e" :answer-field/choices ["q-29664/selection/c3e74ce6fba7b7bd77c7afbf20a82e8f73f6cac3363858b8840c65664e418e03" "q-29664/selection/5172a7fa160e878951e4187c255e106b57da0d14098cfe25d30cce3dbd3535c3" "q-29664/selection/48ed8822e91c61d2180f4e9f6c1b03af7fc6ed780f3ab54702820deafad2d24e" "q-29664/selection/93ba270014b2121e0d07324bf08630808d6ed9bfb1e523439708be4e1492051c" "q-29664/selection/af3979f69e3c75cfe7f3145a4d599cb170a6cd2ea2b65bc8aeb427117c37252c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-29664" :question/id #uuid "0fa4866c-44a3-53be-9ea4-1b3c073da31b" :question/math-academy-id "q-29664" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{4}{3})=(-53.130\\,1…)^{∘}≈-{53.130}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-{53.130}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-{53.130}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}-{53.130}^{∘}={126.870}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{53.130}^{∘},{126.870}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n-53.130\\cdot 126.870≈-6\\,741\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-29664/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-29664"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76930/selection/4119d9791dd34a94cf398689c6a336951707fa106c4ae36ea281d788f4085a50" :answer/id #uuid "9fdaf85f-52f4-5772-b936-fa11de57bf3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76930/selection/908d1d9015b4446f7f4613238506c21dfacedd5aec03544c694256ff366be30b" :answer/id #uuid "4493d027-4c78-5dc8-a9f9-908208ae85a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76930/selection/2cb480d9a9851c91c435bbf82cedd4c11933f683c1bce4e019ea5a5030b4cee1" :answer/id #uuid "36d35d45-42ed-52cb-bd79-633b935f1cb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76930/selection/9a81b9a751ef7286c86058e7839360eb7eeb64cfda43fd381fa54b765e0e3687" :answer/id #uuid "f501b76f-6542-541e-b055-b326f9fb0f32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76930/selection/65cca031fb0c3d58e669bcbabbe43f8d54e4350ed6e256d1f00fa76238fc83d6" :answer/id #uuid "5952884d-c0bc-5192-91c3-3262c4e76705" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76930/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b576ac6-7d2d-5d8b-a5e0-a730d875bb02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76930/selection/65cca031fb0c3d58e669bcbabbe43f8d54e4350ed6e256d1f00fa76238fc83d6" :answer-field/choices ["q-76930/selection/4119d9791dd34a94cf398689c6a336951707fa106c4ae36ea281d788f4085a50" "q-76930/selection/908d1d9015b4446f7f4613238506c21dfacedd5aec03544c694256ff366be30b" "q-76930/selection/2cb480d9a9851c91c435bbf82cedd4c11933f683c1bce4e019ea5a5030b4cee1" "q-76930/selection/9a81b9a751ef7286c86058e7839360eb7eeb64cfda43fd381fa54b765e0e3687" "q-76930/selection/65cca031fb0c3d58e669bcbabbe43f8d54e4350ed6e256d1f00fa76238fc83d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76930" :question/id #uuid "5ccb0e75-04a0-58e0-9f38-1667d6f95491" :question/math-academy-id "q-76930" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=5\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(5)=1.373\\,400…≈1.373\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.373\\,4$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.373\\,4.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi +1.373\\,4=4.515\\,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n1.373\\,4\\cdot 4.515\\,0≈6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-76930/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c71c49ee-e496-5d4e-87fa-96e28dd830ca"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76930"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120646/selection/76a2e7a61a701c25588fb722989f7e3f4b8f1fb17904d20c0f4d6892b152fb4f" :answer/id #uuid "95fad9b7-23d8-597b-9738-7b546b777c57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\,200" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120646/selection/f7b7dc3e66db343857e62e4dc0071629ceb9440cac238c287cb7d0705426b7bd" :answer/id #uuid "ea120bf4-0df9-5da9-9be8-0e2dcce6b7ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,900" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120646/selection/bf68b941a1cdc58fe4bedadfb75da886a393834a0441e100b32da570fd12ef03" :answer/id #uuid "cd48009c-ccfe-52a1-8bd2-2af1b51fc429" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120646/selection/6efe6b401a4d91c44b13aac27c5edcf1a917fdf1abcd99d0018dadf99821fdf1" :answer/id #uuid "3f6ffdfa-f8d5-5191-80b1-e101f7e36314" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120646/selection/799c5c84e661ba1e51984b97e580f9583290bba0f88284f5fad7c69241ce6f3a" :answer/id #uuid "67846b5d-c609-50dc-9bca-2d657876d202" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "37\\,800" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120646/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "56dde13a-0670-549d-b0b5-63bfaa05e94b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120646/selection/6efe6b401a4d91c44b13aac27c5edcf1a917fdf1abcd99d0018dadf99821fdf1" :answer-field/choices ["q-120646/selection/76a2e7a61a701c25588fb722989f7e3f4b8f1fb17904d20c0f4d6892b152fb4f" "q-120646/selection/f7b7dc3e66db343857e62e4dc0071629ceb9440cac238c287cb7d0705426b7bd" "q-120646/selection/bf68b941a1cdc58fe4bedadfb75da886a393834a0441e100b32da570fd12ef03" "q-120646/selection/6efe6b401a4d91c44b13aac27c5edcf1a917fdf1abcd99d0018dadf99821fdf1" "q-120646/selection/799c5c84e661ba1e51984b97e580f9583290bba0f88284f5fad7c69241ce6f3a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120646" :question/id #uuid "1e429c53-db66-50b4-900e-8aac7266bdd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120646" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $7-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120646/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120646"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-330708/field-1/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77" :answer/id #uuid "3a33c542-bcce-5088-aa86-76ed383a760b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330708/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "94902314-a3df-52b9-8de1-513711ccceec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330708/field-1/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77" :answer-field/choices ["q-330708/field-1/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330708/field-2/82a873b6b3ece3b4059b2da1839886387bbd4d4e56d0ad136fc169204f9d3030" :answer/id #uuid "9373aa94-e594-5875-840b-af112ff604a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330708/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d65bd88b-96d1-5564-b9d4-1fd3cb386455" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330708/field-2/82a873b6b3ece3b4059b2da1839886387bbd4d4e56d0ad136fc169204f9d3030" :answer-field/choices ["q-330708/field-2/82a873b6b3ece3b4059b2da1839886387bbd4d4e56d0ad136fc169204f9d3030"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330708" :question/id #uuid "f29a6dbd-b262-5fdc-930e-f2d2316607f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-330708" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\sqrt{8-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}=\\sqrt{8}$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\ 8-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =8 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-330708/field-1" "q-330708/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-330708"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
